WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.985
- Hey everybody.

00:00:00.985 --> 00:00:03.402
Today, we are talking pasta.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:05.007
- [Alexis] The best.

00:00:05.007 --> 00:00:07.503
- We are going around Los Angeles

00:00:07.503 --> 00:00:09.857
to each of our favorite spots

00:00:09.857 --> 00:00:13.587
for the tastiest pasta dish in all of LA.

00:00:13.587 --> 00:00:16.337
(big band music)

00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:31.463
- I really, really love pasta.

00:00:31.463 --> 00:00:32.476
Who doesn't want a big bowl of pasta

00:00:32.476 --> 00:00:34.201
when you come home from work, am I right?

00:00:34.201 --> 00:00:36.582
The tastiest pasta in LA
is at Scopa Italian Roots

00:00:36.582 --> 00:00:39.599
in Venice, and it is
the rigatoni a la vodka.

00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:41.496
Chef Antonia follows the Italian rule,

00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:43.178
which is to say, less is more.

00:00:43.178 --> 00:00:45.518
It's really well balanced,
you get a little spice,

00:00:45.518 --> 00:00:47.557
you get this pepperiness from the garlic,

00:00:47.557 --> 00:00:49.489
you get creaminess from the cheeses.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:50.623
There's whole tomatoes in it

00:00:50.623 --> 00:00:53.162
to add this really great texture.

00:00:53.162 --> 00:00:54.503
The rigatoni has these great ridges

00:00:54.503 --> 00:00:58.661
which collect the sauce, and
it makes it the perfect dish.

00:00:58.661 --> 00:01:00.301
When you first take that bite of rigatoni,

00:01:00.301 --> 00:01:03.239
the sauce kind of washes over you.

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:06.070
It feels like Chef Antonia
is giving you a hug.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.959
- I'm Antonia Lofaso,
I'm the executive chef

00:01:07.959 --> 00:01:09.349
here at Scopa Italian Roots,

00:01:09.349 --> 00:01:12.336
and I'm going to make
my rigatoni a la vodka.

00:01:12.336 --> 00:01:14.359
Here, we're gonna start
with a little bit of butter,

00:01:14.359 --> 00:01:17.312
and a little bit of
extra virgin olive oil.

00:01:17.312 --> 00:01:19.578
What the olive oil is
going to do is add flavor,

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:23.994
but B, it's not going to allow
the butter to cook as brown.

00:01:23.994 --> 00:01:26.208
I don't want it to brown,
I kinda want it to melt.

00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:27.606
Instead of doing a chopped garlic,

00:01:27.606 --> 00:01:29.781
we do three blanched garlic, so--

00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:30.645
- [Alexis] Oh, we blanch them?

00:01:30.645 --> 00:01:32.927
- We blanch the garlic, because
then it makes it sweet--

00:01:32.927 --> 00:01:33.760
- Interesting--

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.501
- Instead of making it
spicy, and garlicky.

00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:37.133
And then, we're gonna add
a little bit of chili.

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:39.329
You wanna add chili
and garlic to fat only,

00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:42.440
because you really want to make sure

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.924
that it blooms, and it doesn't boil.

00:01:44.924 --> 00:01:46.662
We're gonna add a little
bit of the vodka to that.

00:01:46.662 --> 00:01:48.181
Then I'm gonna add cherry tomatoes.

00:01:48.181 --> 00:01:49.564
And I want these to break down,

00:01:49.564 --> 00:01:51.612
but then I do want them a little whole.

00:01:51.612 --> 00:01:53.572
You're adding more fresh tomato flavor,

00:01:53.572 --> 00:01:54.806
because you've got a
cooked tomato product,

00:01:54.806 --> 00:01:56.352
and then a fresh tomato product.

00:01:56.352 --> 00:01:57.871
We're gonna add a little bit of salt,

00:01:57.871 --> 00:01:59.304
and then we're gonna build our sauce.

00:01:59.304 --> 00:02:02.456
We're gonna add a little
bit of san marzano tomato.

00:02:02.456 --> 00:02:04.796
We're gonna add some cream.

00:02:04.796 --> 00:02:06.585
And then we're gonna add just a little bit

00:02:06.585 --> 00:02:07.804
of chicken stock to thin it out,

00:02:07.804 --> 00:02:11.120
because we don't want this
to be an overly chunky sauce.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.238
Then we also want the
pasta to cook in there.

00:02:14.238 --> 00:02:15.413
So let that come together.

00:02:15.413 --> 00:02:16.276
Then we're trying to push that

00:02:16.276 --> 00:02:17.879
off to the side, and let it sit.

00:02:17.879 --> 00:02:18.792
Then we're gonna cook our pasta.

00:02:18.792 --> 00:02:19.625
- Great.

00:02:19.625 --> 00:02:22.094
- This pasta, because
it's a fresh product,

00:02:22.094 --> 00:02:23.312
it's gonna take a lot less,

00:02:23.312 --> 00:02:25.496
but it's gonna be about four,
five minutes in the water.

00:02:25.496 --> 00:02:26.415
I feel like she should be here

00:02:26.415 --> 00:02:27.492
with a glass of wine, enjoying--

00:02:27.492 --> 00:02:28.325
- I mean...

00:02:28.325 --> 00:02:30.736
- So, a little bit of
pasta water goes in there--

00:02:30.736 --> 00:02:31.569
- [Alexis] Okay, so only a few drops.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:32.678
- Only a few drops.

00:02:32.678 --> 00:02:34.949
I can always get it to come
back like that if I need it.

00:02:34.949 --> 00:02:37.823
Now we're gonna take the Parmesan-regiano.

00:02:37.823 --> 00:02:39.927
Then, what I was saying before,

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:42.760
is just adding some fresh parsley,

00:02:43.656 --> 00:02:45.775
and then some fresh petite basil to it.

00:02:45.775 --> 00:02:48.086
And I want to take it off the heat now.

00:02:48.086 --> 00:02:52.195
I'm gonna let it wilt in
there, just like that.

00:02:52.195 --> 00:02:53.945
It's hot, be careful.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.213
Hands down, we won?

00:02:58.213 --> 00:02:59.250
- We're done.

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.057
- [Antonia] So, just when
you thought it was over--

00:03:01.057 --> 00:03:01.890
- I feel like I know what's coming

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.079
and I feel really excited.

00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:05.610
- (laughs) You do know what's coming.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.200
Just a little bit of ricotta,
it's gonna go right on top.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.517
Again, it's all about the small touches.

00:03:10.517 --> 00:03:11.350
- Oh, wow.

00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:14.902
- A little bit of maldon on
each piece of the ricotta,

00:03:14.902 --> 00:03:17.156
and then a little bit of the olive oil.

00:03:17.156 --> 00:03:21.134
I'm gonna do a little bit
of fresh petite basil.

00:03:21.134 --> 00:03:22.717
There's your pasta.

00:03:23.898 --> 00:03:24.731
- [Nick] Probably the prettiest thing

00:03:24.731 --> 00:03:25.822
that's ever been before me.

00:03:25.822 --> 00:03:27.082
- You're just saying that.

00:03:27.082 --> 00:03:28.665
You didn't pick me.

00:03:30.423 --> 00:03:31.598
- But he might now.

00:03:31.598 --> 00:03:34.628
We're going in.
- We gonna do this?

00:03:34.628 --> 00:03:35.461
- I feel like a lot of the time

00:03:35.461 --> 00:03:38.474
when I have vodka sauce,
the tomato gets really lost

00:03:38.474 --> 00:03:40.871
in the cream, but this is perfect.

00:03:40.871 --> 00:03:43.872
You get these pieces of tomato in here.

00:03:43.872 --> 00:03:44.705
- The whole tomato really

00:03:44.705 --> 00:03:46.562
builds out the dish.
- You don't lose that acidity.

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:48.185
It pops really nicely.

00:03:48.185 --> 00:03:49.871
- There's a lot going on for sure.

00:03:49.871 --> 00:03:54.520
It all comes together and,
like, has a baby in your mouth.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:56.404
- Also the basil adds some freshness.

00:03:56.404 --> 00:03:58.901
It's really exceptional.

00:03:58.901 --> 00:04:00.507
- They're nailing that rigatoni, oh boy.

00:04:00.507 --> 00:04:01.639
- Like (bleep)

00:04:01.639 --> 00:04:02.774
- I would like some more.

00:04:02.774 --> 00:04:04.991
Scopa was a beautiful restaurant,

00:04:04.991 --> 00:04:08.848
and that rigatoni a la
vodka was delicious.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:12.314
But it is not the tastiest
pasta in Los Angeles.

00:04:12.314 --> 00:04:13.948
The tastiest pasta in LA is

00:04:13.948 --> 00:04:15.858
the tortellini in brodo al contrario

00:04:15.858 --> 00:04:19.621
being served by chef
Zach Pollack at Alimento.

00:04:19.621 --> 00:04:20.653
For those who aren't familiar,

00:04:20.653 --> 00:04:23.222
there is a Chinese soup
dumpling called xiaolongbao.

00:04:23.222 --> 00:04:26.296
What Chef Zach has done
is taken all these flavors

00:04:26.296 --> 00:04:30.125
of this classic Italian
dish, and he has put them

00:04:30.125 --> 00:04:32.506
into this form of a soup dumpling.

00:04:32.506 --> 00:04:33.440
The first thing that happens

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.550
when you bite into the
tortellini is there's this rush

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.022
of hot broth into your mouth
that pops out of the pasta.

00:04:40.022 --> 00:04:44.222
You get these flavors of
Parmesan, prosciutto, chicken,

00:04:44.222 --> 00:04:47.672
butter, it's all there
and it all works together.

00:04:47.672 --> 00:04:50.590
It's not like anything else
you've ever had before.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.188
- I'm Zach Pollack, and
I'm the chef of Alimento

00:04:53.188 --> 00:04:54.425
in Silver Lake, and today we're making

00:04:54.425 --> 00:04:56.986
tortellini in brodo al contrario.

00:04:56.986 --> 00:04:59.723
So we're gonna start by rolling
out our egg dough pasta.

00:04:59.723 --> 00:05:01.620
Got a little, nifty machine here.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.270
- [Mike] Is this a
fairly traditional pasta,

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.758
you're not reinventing the wheel here?

00:05:04.758 --> 00:05:05.950
- We're definitely not
reinventing the wheel.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:10.174
It's egg-heavy in the sort
of Northern Italian style.

00:05:10.174 --> 00:05:12.367
There's a combination
of whole eggs and yolks.

00:05:12.367 --> 00:05:14.143
We're gonna knead it through a few times

00:05:14.143 --> 00:05:16.848
so we get the right texture.

00:05:16.848 --> 00:05:18.049
Now we got that.

00:05:18.049 --> 00:05:19.962
Put a little bit of flour on the bottom.

00:05:19.962 --> 00:05:21.216
We have our filling here--

00:05:21.216 --> 00:05:22.807
- Okay, so what is in this magical tube?

00:05:22.807 --> 00:05:25.744
- There is a really rich brodo.

00:05:25.744 --> 00:05:28.863
It's stock made from
chicken feet, chicken bones,

00:05:28.863 --> 00:05:30.985
beef shank, and prosciutto hocks.

00:05:30.985 --> 00:05:32.644
- [Mike] Not just chicken stock.

00:05:32.644 --> 00:05:34.391
- Not just chicken stock, no.

00:05:34.391 --> 00:05:35.875
Now we fold this over.

00:05:35.875 --> 00:05:37.458
Seal it on the sides first.

00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:41.108
Now I'm gonna go seal the
top part, but as I do,

00:05:41.108 --> 00:05:42.748
so I'm not pushing any more air in,

00:05:42.748 --> 00:05:44.027
I'm gonna put my fingers on the side.

00:05:44.027 --> 00:05:45.067
Now we're gonna cut it,

00:05:45.067 --> 00:05:46.829
and now we'll do the
same thing on the top.

00:05:46.829 --> 00:05:49.019
If I align them right, I'll be able to

00:05:49.019 --> 00:05:51.480
just make one cut through both of them.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.478
Now we're gonna shape them.

00:05:52.478 --> 00:05:56.543
We just wrap them similar to
making an actual tortellino,

00:05:56.543 --> 00:05:59.587
where you might wrap it
around a smaller digit.

00:05:59.587 --> 00:06:01.027
In this case it's a bigger noodle,

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:01.992
we wrap it around the thumb.

00:06:01.992 --> 00:06:03.161
I'm gonna start the butter sauce here.

00:06:03.161 --> 00:06:06.055
It consists of butter and water.

00:06:06.055 --> 00:06:07.832
You know, we'll season
it with a little salt--

00:06:07.832 --> 00:06:08.665
- That's all you need.

00:06:08.665 --> 00:06:11.082
- A little bit of water here.

00:06:12.565 --> 00:06:14.021
We'll move that off the heat for a second.

00:06:14.021 --> 00:06:15.324
We'll cook our pasta.

00:06:15.324 --> 00:06:17.587
Give it a little stir so
nothing sticks to the bottom.

00:06:17.587 --> 00:06:19.740
That'll probably take
about two minutes, maybe.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:20.992
- [Mike] And then what do we have here?

00:06:20.992 --> 00:06:22.448
- Here is our prosciutto and pan sauce.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:23.951
This is dried porcini mushrooms

00:06:23.951 --> 00:06:25.201
that have cooked down with shallots,

00:06:25.201 --> 00:06:28.010
some diced prosciutto cotto,
Italian ham, basically.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.358
We're floating, which is good.

00:06:29.358 --> 00:06:30.297
- Right into the butter sauce.

00:06:30.297 --> 00:06:31.632
- Right into the butter sauce.

00:06:31.632 --> 00:06:32.872
We'll finish them gently in the sauce.

00:06:32.872 --> 00:06:33.757
Like I said, it's not one

00:06:33.757 --> 00:06:35.149
of those pastas you want to beat up.

00:06:35.149 --> 00:06:37.607
Okay, now we'll add our
cheese off the heat.

00:06:37.607 --> 00:06:40.796
You never want to add cheese
on heat, it becomes stringy.

00:06:40.796 --> 00:06:43.008
Put down a little bit of the sauce.

00:06:43.008 --> 00:06:44.382
It's pretty rich, so not too much.

00:06:44.382 --> 00:06:45.544
And now, the pasta.

00:06:45.544 --> 00:06:46.377
- [Mike] You guys hungry yet?

00:06:46.377 --> 00:06:47.640
- Drooling.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.944
- [Zach] And now we'll finish
with a little grated Parm.

00:06:49.944 --> 00:06:51.185
And you gotta eat these while they're hot,

00:06:51.185 --> 00:06:53.590
so I suggest you all take your seats.

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:54.712
- Alright, you guys ready for this?

00:06:54.712 --> 00:06:56.545
- I mean no, but yeah.

00:06:59.832 --> 00:07:00.832
- Oh my God.

00:07:01.685 --> 00:07:02.718
- Oh my God.

00:07:02.718 --> 00:07:04.680
- Honestly, when it just explodes

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:07.029
in your mouth, I don't think I've ever had

00:07:07.029 --> 00:07:09.131
anything happen to me like that before.

00:07:09.131 --> 00:07:11.484
- The thing is, don't even have
to bite it, it's so tender.

00:07:11.484 --> 00:07:12.317
It just--

00:07:12.317 --> 00:07:13.150
- You pop 'em.

00:07:13.150 --> 00:07:14.537
- Yeah, you can kind of
pop it with your tongue--

00:07:14.537 --> 00:07:15.370
- And then--

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.490
- And then you get the chew of the pasta.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:18.430
- You get the chew, but then
it goes all over your mouth,

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:22.473
and it's this salty,
savory swath of goodness.

00:07:22.473 --> 00:07:24.091
- And it's funny, it's deceptive.

00:07:24.091 --> 00:07:27.653
- I've always loved food
that's got a little secret.

00:07:27.653 --> 00:07:28.486
Does that make sense?

00:07:28.486 --> 00:07:29.319
- Yeah.

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:30.152
- It does.

00:07:30.152 --> 00:07:32.223
I only wanna eat this pasta
for the rest of my life.

00:07:32.223 --> 00:07:33.592
- [Crew Member] Don't finish it.

00:07:33.592 --> 00:07:34.461
- What the (bleep), dude?

00:07:34.461 --> 00:07:35.326
- Yeah, they need to eat now.

00:07:35.326 --> 00:07:36.159
- Oh is it for you guys?

00:07:36.159 --> 00:07:36.992
- [Crew Member] Yes.

00:07:36.992 --> 00:07:37.825
- Oh, sorry sorry sorry sorry sorry.

00:07:37.825 --> 00:07:38.658
- Try it.

00:07:38.658 --> 00:07:40.017
Dude, get in here.

00:07:40.017 --> 00:07:41.815
Darling, the tortellini...

00:07:41.815 --> 00:07:45.302
Oh my God, I'm still trying to recover.

00:07:45.302 --> 00:07:47.684
But, we're off to my place now.

00:07:47.684 --> 00:07:50.785
The tastiest pasta in
Los Angeles, California

00:07:50.785 --> 00:07:52.433
is the spaghetti and meatballs

00:07:52.433 --> 00:07:55.882
at Little Dom's in Los Feliz, Los Angeles.

00:07:55.882 --> 00:08:00.305
When you take a bite of this
amazing spaghetti and meatballs

00:08:00.305 --> 00:08:03.472
you have that delicious al dente pasta

00:08:04.826 --> 00:08:06.951
that's gonna give you that nice bite.

00:08:06.951 --> 00:08:11.464
And then the simplicity of
that amazing tomato sauce.

00:08:11.464 --> 00:08:13.583
How can I forget about that meat?

00:08:13.583 --> 00:08:17.328
Made with only beef and pork,
the fat from both of those.

00:08:17.328 --> 00:08:21.511
Oh, it all comes together and
makes the most fabulous dish.

00:08:21.511 --> 00:08:23.395
Hi, I'm Brandon Boudet, the chef and owner

00:08:23.395 --> 00:08:25.076
of Little Dom's Restaurant,

00:08:25.076 --> 00:08:26.459
and I'm gonna show you how to make

00:08:26.459 --> 00:08:28.790
Little Dom's spaghetti and meatballs.

00:08:28.790 --> 00:08:32.029
We're extruding some fresh
pasta, making some spaghetti.

00:08:32.029 --> 00:08:33.412
All it is is two ingredients.

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:34.580
Can you guess?

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:36.371
- Flour and eggs.

00:08:36.371 --> 00:08:37.204
- (mimics buzzer)

00:08:37.204 --> 00:08:38.037
- [Nick] Or water?

00:08:38.037 --> 00:08:39.787
- Water, it's semolina flour and water

00:08:39.787 --> 00:08:41.895
to make a nice hardy, sturdy spaghetti.

00:08:41.895 --> 00:08:42.728
- [Nick] That's it?

00:08:42.728 --> 00:08:43.561
- Yup.

00:08:43.561 --> 00:08:45.790
We use imported Italian
San Marzano tomatoes.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:47.289
All we do is rice them,

00:08:47.289 --> 00:08:49.156
meaning that we put them
through our food mill.

00:08:49.156 --> 00:08:49.989
Here, you wanna try it?

00:08:49.989 --> 00:08:50.822
- [Nick] Do I just pour it in?

00:08:50.822 --> 00:08:51.655
- Yeah, just dump it in.

00:08:51.655 --> 00:08:52.488
We like to do it this way.

00:08:52.488 --> 00:08:53.662
It doesn't incorporate
a bunch of air into it,

00:08:53.662 --> 00:08:56.350
if you threw it into a food
processor or a blender.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:09:00.623
We take basil, oregano,
a little bit of garlic,

00:09:00.623 --> 00:09:02.018
and we put that in a little sachet,

00:09:02.018 --> 00:09:04.778
and simmer this all for about three hours.

00:09:04.778 --> 00:09:06.313
Our meatballs are pretty straightforward.

00:09:06.313 --> 00:09:07.951
I like a mixture of pork and beef.

00:09:07.951 --> 00:09:10.112
But the one little trick
that we like to put in there

00:09:10.112 --> 00:09:11.653
is a little porcini powder.

00:09:11.653 --> 00:09:13.215
Gives it that little earthiness.

00:09:13.215 --> 00:09:15.819
We just like to do it very simple.

00:09:15.819 --> 00:09:20.246
Just like this, round it off,
one, two, three, ready to go.

00:09:20.246 --> 00:09:21.486
- [Nick] So about how
long do you bake 'em for?

00:09:21.486 --> 00:09:23.063
- Right around 25 minutes.

00:09:23.063 --> 00:09:24.563
At a low 275, 325.

00:09:25.851 --> 00:09:27.849
- [Nick] And then the magic happens.

00:09:27.849 --> 00:09:28.682
- [Brandon] Yeah, the magic.

00:09:28.682 --> 00:09:30.792
- [Nick] Damn, nice.

00:09:30.792 --> 00:09:33.465
- That same meatball mixture
we use to make meat sauce.

00:09:33.465 --> 00:09:35.762
So take a couple spoonfuls,
throw it in there,

00:09:35.762 --> 00:09:38.124
saute up that meat,
break it up a little bit.

00:09:38.124 --> 00:09:41.817
Then all we're gonna do is
add that tomato sauce to it.

00:09:41.817 --> 00:09:43.067
- [Nick] How long does this cook for?

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:44.392
- Three hours.

00:09:44.392 --> 00:09:45.452
The pasta we just made, okay?

00:09:45.452 --> 00:09:47.956
Going into some hot, boiling water.

00:09:47.956 --> 00:09:50.866
It's probably only gonna
take four, five minutes, max.

00:09:50.866 --> 00:09:52.380
We're gonna grab some meat sauce.

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:53.904
- [Nick] I see some meatballs in there.

00:09:53.904 --> 00:09:54.798
- Yup.

00:09:54.798 --> 00:09:56.635
A little bit of butter to it.

00:09:56.635 --> 00:09:57.769
Check on the pasta.

00:09:57.769 --> 00:09:59.410
Yeah, it's totally done.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:01.077
- Look at that flip.

00:10:02.293 --> 00:10:04.368
- [Brandon] The magic, that baby is done,

00:10:04.368 --> 00:10:06.535
ready to go into the bowl.

00:10:09.418 --> 00:10:10.827
- This is just what we needed.

00:10:10.827 --> 00:10:14.294
- It's no (bleep), it's straight
up spaghetti and meatballs.

00:10:14.294 --> 00:10:15.877
- It makes me jealous
that I didn't grow up

00:10:15.877 --> 00:10:17.710
in a big Italian family.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.627
- The noodles, too, so perfect.

00:10:19.627 --> 00:10:20.547
- Perfectly cooked.

00:10:20.547 --> 00:10:23.257
The sauce, it's really well-balanced.

00:10:23.257 --> 00:10:26.573
- The sauce, the cheese, the meat.

00:10:26.573 --> 00:10:28.434
- Its strength is in
its simplicity, right?

00:10:28.434 --> 00:10:30.309
Not to say that there's no subtlety in it.

00:10:30.309 --> 00:10:33.382
There's a little kick
of pepper underneath--

00:10:33.382 --> 00:10:34.215
- [Nick] Oh yeah

00:10:34.215 --> 00:10:35.606
- You can feel there's a little
bit of heat in that sauce.

00:10:35.606 --> 00:10:36.439
- Totally.

00:10:36.439 --> 00:10:38.854
- There's the tiniest hint
of the herbs in the meatball,

00:10:38.854 --> 00:10:40.100
but it's not overwhelming.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:41.697
- Also, the size of the meatballs.

00:10:41.697 --> 00:10:45.607
Too big, overwhelming,
too small, underwhelming.

00:10:45.607 --> 00:10:47.778
- Perfect medium size.

00:10:47.778 --> 00:10:50.928
And you wash it down with this
delicious Little Dom's wine.

00:10:50.928 --> 00:10:52.299
- This is the hardest one for me so far.

00:10:52.299 --> 00:10:53.220
- I don't know what we're gonna do.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:54.053
- [Nick] Me neither.

00:10:54.053 --> 00:10:55.858
- Little Dom's was so good.

00:10:55.858 --> 00:10:57.021
- [Alexis] It felt like
you were going home.

00:10:57.021 --> 00:10:58.608
- [Mike] I do think that Scopa

00:10:58.608 --> 00:11:00.458
sort of hitting similar notes.

00:11:00.458 --> 00:11:02.154
- [Nick] So creamy.

00:11:02.154 --> 00:11:03.300
- It was an elevated version

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:05.447
of what rigatoni al vodka could be.

00:11:05.447 --> 00:11:08.369
- I think the tie-breaker
for me has to be,

00:11:08.369 --> 00:11:11.493
not only was that tortellini
absolutely delicious

00:11:11.493 --> 00:11:14.603
and perfectly done, but it's
exciting, it's different.

00:11:14.603 --> 00:11:15.728
So I'm sticking with Alimento.

00:11:15.728 --> 00:11:16.925
- I mean--
- What are you thinking?

00:11:16.925 --> 00:11:18.874
- Go to all three of these
restaurants, first of all.

00:11:18.874 --> 00:11:19.759
- Absolutely.

00:11:19.759 --> 00:11:21.394
- We were floored.

00:11:21.394 --> 00:11:22.235
- [Nick] We were.

00:11:22.235 --> 00:11:25.241
- We were floored, I was speechless.

00:11:25.241 --> 00:11:26.514
- [Together] Alimento?

00:11:26.514 --> 00:11:28.065
- Yes!

00:11:28.065 --> 00:11:30.065
- Alimento. Eh, finally.

00:11:32.057 --> 00:11:33.327
- Shout out to Zach, honey,

00:11:33.327 --> 00:11:34.240
'cause whatever you're putting

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:37.328
in them damn noodles child, keep doing it.

00:11:37.328 --> 00:11:38.744
'Cause you're doing it right.

00:11:38.744 --> 00:11:41.494
(big band music)

